'''Belljardí''' is a city in the [[Cumará (comarca)|Cumará comarca]], [[Estret Province]], [[Balboa]]. With a population of 217,456 in 2026, it is the fourth-largest municipality in Cumará. Belljardí is an affluent residential suburb known for its extensive parks, landscaped boulevards, and low-rise housing stock, situated between [[Enllaç]] to the north and [[Varakí]] to the southeast. The city functions as a commuter suburb for both [[Sant Cristòfor]] and the [[International City of Areza|International City of Areza]], with residents relying primarily on expressway connections and feeder bus services to reach rail stations in neighbouring municipalities.

The city's name means "beautiful garden" in Volisanian, derived from the landscaped estates and gardens that characterised the area before its residential development. The name predates the municipality's formal establishment, referring originally to a country estate whose ornamental grounds gave the surrounding area its identity.

== Geography ==

Belljardí occupies 84.8 km² on the flat lowlands of the southern Estret corridor, positioned between the [[Enllaç]] commuter corridor to the north and the [[Varakí]] lakefront district to the southeast. The municipality borders [[Enllaç]] to the north, [[La Cruïlla]] to the northeast, [[Varakí]] to the southeast, [[Els Horts]] to the south, and [[Pla dels Horts]] to the west. The terrain is generally flat, with a gradual, barely perceptible rise from west to east.

[[Expressway 1 (Balboa)|Expressway 1]] runs north–south through the eastern portion of the municipality, providing a direct connection to Sant Cristòfor to the north and to the Varakí and Areza corridor to the south. [[Expressway 68 (Balboa)|Expressway 68]] crosses the southern portion of the city from west to east, intersecting E1 and creating the road junction that defines the southern edge of the built-up area. The Portnou Subdivision rail line passes along the western edge of the municipality, but does not stop within Belljardí; the nearest CristoCarril Line B stations are in neighbouring [[Enllaç]].

The city's street pattern is less regular than the grid layouts of Enllaç or La Cruïlla, reflecting its development as a planned garden suburb rather than a rail corridor settlement. Curving residential streets, tree-lined boulevards, and intermittent green spaces characterise the urban form, with higher-density housing clustered along the expressway frontage roads and the main commercial corridors.

=== Parks and green spaces ===

Belljardí's park network is the defining feature of the city's urban character and the source of its name. The municipal parks system includes several large parks and numerous smaller green spaces distributed throughout the residential neighbourhoods. The central park — the Parc Central de Belljardí, a landscaped expanse of lawns, tree-lined paths, and ornamental gardens in the geographic centre of the city — occupies the site of the original country estate that gave the municipality its name. The park serves as the civic and recreational heart of the city, containing the municipal government offices, the central library, and the principal community facilities.

Additional parks are distributed throughout the residential zones, connected by tree-lined boulevards and cycling paths that form a continuous green network. The park system was expanded progressively as the city grew, with each new residential development required to dedicate land for public green space. The result is a higher proportion of parkland per capita than any other municipality in the Cumará comarca.

=== Climate ===

Belljardí has a {{w|tropical monsoon climate}} (Köppen ''Am''), consistent with the southern Estret lowlands. Temperatures are warm year-round, with average daily highs of 30–32 °C and lows of 22–24 °C. The wet season runs from May to November, with the heaviest rainfall from June to August. The extensive tree canopy and park network provide local microclimate moderation, with slightly cooler daytime temperatures in the park-adjacent neighbourhoods compared to the expressway corridors.

== History ==

=== Zong Balboa period (1899–1950) ===

{{main|Zong Balboa}}

Before the residential development of the area, the land that became Belljardí was occupied by agricultural estates and country properties belonging to wealthy families from [[Sant Cristòfor]]. The largest of these estates — known for its ornamental gardens and landscaped grounds — gave the area its name. The Portnou Line railroad, established during the Zong period to connect Sant Cristòfor to the canal zone, ran along the western edge of the estate lands, but no station was built in the area; the railroad served primarily as a freight line, and the estate owners had no need for a passenger halt.

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947) did not directly affect the Belljardí estates, which were located well south of the fighting in Sant Cristòfor. The agricultural land continued to produce food for the regional market throughout the war years.

=== AC mandate and garden suburb development (1950–1973) ===

{{main|International City of Areza|Treaty of Areza}}

The [[Adonian Community]] mandate period transformed the Belljardí area from agricultural estates to a planned residential suburb. As the construction of the [[International City of Areza]] drew workers and professionals to the southern Estret corridor, the demand for housing in the area between Sant Cristòfor and Areza increased sharply. The estate owners sold their land to developers, and the municipal government — established in 1961 as part of the post-reunification territorial reorganisation — adopted a garden suburb plan that preserved significant portions of the original estate grounds as public parks.

The garden suburb plan distinguished Belljardí from the denser, rail-corridor suburbs of Enllaç and La Cruïlla. Instead of concentrating apartment blocks along a rail line, the plan called for lower-density residential development organised around parks and green spaces, with the existing estate gardens preserved as the core of the municipal park network. The plan attracted affluent buyers — professionals, civil servants, and merchants — who sought a quieter, greener residential environment within commuting distance of Sant Cristòfor.

The [[Treaty of Areza]] (1973) accelerated the city's growth by opening the corridor to private investment and improving road connections. The construction of [[Expressway 1 (Balboa)|Expressway 1]] through the eastern portion of the municipality provided a direct road connection to Sant Cristòfor, making Belljardí attractive to commuters who preferred to drive rather than rely on rail.

=== Post-reunification growth (1973–present) ===

The post-reunification period saw sustained residential growth, driven by the city's reputation as an affluent, green suburb. The construction of [[Expressway 68 (Balboa)|Expressway 68]] across the southern portion of the municipality in the 1980s improved east–west connectivity and opened new land for development. Residential development in the 1980s and 1990s expanded the city outward from the central park core, with mid-rise apartment buildings and townhouse developments along the expressway frontage roads and detached housing in the interior neighbourhoods.

By the 2000s, Belljardí had established itself as one of the most desirable residential municipalities in the Cumará comarca, with property values among the highest in the southern Estret corridor. The city's growth has been slower and more controlled than that of Enllaç or La Cruïlla, reflecting the garden suburb planning tradition and the municipal government's emphasis on maintaining the park character of the city.

== Government ==

Belljardí is governed by an elected {{w|mayor}} and a municipal council. The mayor serves four-year terms and has executive authority over municipal administration, planning, and local services. The current mayor is [[Martí Vidal i Castelló]] of the [[Liberal Party of Balboa]] (PLB), elected in 2024. Vidal i Castelló, a former municipal parks commissioner, campaigned on preserving the city's green character, managing development pressure along the expressway corridors, and improving bus connections to regional rail services. The PLB's dominance in Belljardí reflects the city's affluent, property-owning electorate and the residents' preference for controlled development and municipal autonomy.

== Demographics ==

Belljardí's population of 217,456 (2026) makes it the fourth-largest municipality in the Cumará comarca. The population is affluent relative to the comarca average, with higher household incomes, larger dwelling sizes, and a higher rate of vehicle ownership. The city's demographic profile differs from the rail-corridor suburbs of Enllaç and La Cruïlla: the population is older, with a larger proportion of established families and retired professionals, and the household size is slightly smaller, consistent with the prevalence of single-family and low-rise housing.

The ethnic composition reflects the city's affluent character. [[White Balboan people|White Balboans]] and [[Mixed Balboan people|mixed Balboans]] constitute the majority of the population, distributed throughout the residential neighbourhoods. [[Black Balboan people|Black Balboans]] are present in smaller numbers than in the rail-corridor suburbs, concentrated in the apartment developments along the expressway frontage roads. The [[Fosian Balboan people|Fosian]] community is small, consisting primarily of professional-class [[Zong Balboan people|Zong]] and [[Sinchewese Balboan people|Sinchew]] Balboans employed in Sant Cristòfor or Areza.

== Economy ==

Belljardí's economy is primarily residential and service-oriented. The city functions as a bedroom community for commuters to Sant Cristòfor and Areza, with a large proportion of the working population commuting by car via the expressway network. Unlike the rail-corridor suburbs, where employment is concentrated around station areas, Belljardí's employment is dispersed — residents work across Sant Cristòfor, Areza, and the broader Estret corridor.

The commercial district around the Parc Central de Belljardí serves as the city's principal retail and service centre, with shops, restaurants, professional offices, and community facilities. Secondary commercial nodes have developed along the expressway frontage roads, particularly at the E1–E68 interchange, where retail and service businesses cater to both local residents and pass-through traffic. The city has minimal industrial activity, limited to small-scale service businesses and home-based professional practices.

== Transport ==

Belljardí's transport network is built around road connections, as the municipality lacks direct rail or metro service. The city relies on expressway access and feeder bus services to connect residents to the regional rail network.

=== Commuter rail ===

Belljardí has no rail station within its municipal boundary. The Portnou Subdivision rail line passes along the western edge of the city, but does not stop. Residents access CristoCarril Line B services via stations in neighbouring [[Enllaç]], the closest being the Carretera de Sant Joan station approximately 2 km from the Belljardí western border. The municipal bus network provides feeder connections to these stations.

=== Bus ===

The ''Autobus de Belljardí'' (AdB) is the municipal bus network operated by the city government. The network consists of five lines radiating from the Parc Central de Belljardí transit hub in the city centre. The lines connect the residential neighbourhoods to the commercial core, the expressway interchange commercial nodes, and the neighbouring rail stations in Enllaç. The AdB's primary function is to provide feeder service to CristoCarril Line B stations in Enllaç, enabling Belljardí residents to commute by rail to Sant Cristòfor and the broader regional network. Selected routes also extend to [[La Cruïlla]] for connections to CristoCarril Line C and Metro Lines 8 and 9.

=== Roads ===

[[Expressway 1 (Balboa)|Expressway 1]] runs north–south through the eastern portion of Belljardí, providing the primary road connection to Sant Cristòfor to the north and to [[Varakí]] and the Areza corridor to the south. [[Expressway 68 (Balboa)|Expressway 68]] crosses the southern portion of the municipality from west to east, intersecting E1 at a major interchange. The E1–E68 interchange is one of the largest road junctions in the southern Estret corridor and serves as a secondary commercial node for the city. Local roads connect the residential neighbourhoods to the expressway interchanges and the neighbouring municipalities, with the tree-lined boulevards of the garden suburb plan serving as the principal arterial streets.

== Neighbourhoods ==

Belljardí is divided into four municipal zones, each reflecting a different period and pattern of the city's growth:

=== Zona 1 — Central ===

'''Zona 1''' is the oldest and most affluent part of the city, centred on the Parc Central de Belljardí and the original garden suburb development. The street pattern follows the curving, tree-lined layout of the original garden suburb plan, with detached housing and low-rise apartment buildings set back from the road behind landscaped frontages. The housing stock consists of a mix of original garden suburb houses from the 1960s and 1970s, newer infill development, and mid-rise apartment blocks along the main boulevards. Zona 1 is the civic and commercial hub of the municipality, containing the municipal government offices, the central library, the principal community facilities, and the main commercial district. The Parc Central de Belljardí dominates the zone, with the surrounding streets oriented toward the park.

=== Zona 2 — Nord ===

'''Zona 2''' is the northern portion of the municipality, bordering [[Enllaç]]. The zone developed in the 1970s and 1980s as the city expanded northward toward the Enllaç commuter corridor. The housing stock consists of mid-rise apartment blocks and townhouse developments, denser than Zona 1 but still lower-density than the rail-corridor suburbs. Zona 2 has the highest proportion of [[Black Balboan people|Black Balboan]] residents in Belljardí, concentrated in the apartment developments near the Enllaç border. The zone's proximity to Enllaç's CristoCarril Line B stations makes it the most transit-accessible part of the city, and the AdB feeder routes to Enllaç are most frequent in this zone.

=== Zona 3 — Est ===

'''Zona 3''' is the eastern portion of the municipality, developed along the [[Expressway 1 (Balboa)|Expressway 1]] corridor. The zone is the newest part of Belljardí, with much of its development dating from the 1990s and 2000s. The housing stock consists of modern townhouse developments and low-rise apartment buildings, laid out along the expressway frontage roads and the connecting local streets. The E1 corridor provides direct road access to Sant Cristòfor and Varakí, making Zona 3 attractive to car commuters. The E1–E68 interchange at the southern edge of the zone has developed as a secondary commercial node, with retail and service businesses serving both local residents and expressway traffic.

=== Zona 4 — Sud ===

'''Zona 4''' is the southern portion of the municipality, bordering [[Els Horts]] and [[Varakí]]. The zone was developed in the 1980s following the construction of [[Expressway 68 (Balboa)|Expressway 68]], which crosses the zone from west to east. The housing stock consists of a mix of detached housing, townhouses, and garden apartments, with larger lot sizes than in the other zones. Zona 4 retains a more suburban character than the rest of Belljardí, with the transition toward the semi-rural municipality of Els Horts gradual rather than sharply defined. The zone's southern edge, along the Varakí border, has seen some recent apartment development taking advantage of the proximity to Varakí's metro and streetcar network.

== Notable places ==

* '''Parc Central de Belljardí''' — the city's central park, occupying the site of the original country estate whose gardens gave the municipality its name. The park contains the municipal government offices, the central library, and the principal community facilities, and serves as the civic and recreational heart of the city.
* '''E1–E68 interchange''' — the major road junction in the southern portion of the municipality, where Expressway 1 and Expressway 68 intersect. The interchange area has developed as a secondary commercial node with retail and service businesses.
* '''Belljardí transit hub''' — the municipal bus interchange at the Parc Central de Belljardí, where all five Autobus de Belljardí lines originate. The hub provides feeder connections to CristoCarril Line B stations in Enllaç and to Metro and CristoCarril Line C services in La Cruïlla.

